{"title":"Ishika's Leaf Colour Mystery at the Garden","gradeSubjectTag":"Class 5 · Science · Photosynthesis","readMinutes":2,"scenes":[{"label":"hook","text":"Ishika was sitting in her grandmother's garden on a bright morning, looking at all the green leaves on the neem tree. 'Dadi, why are these leaves so green?' she asked. Her grandmother smiled and said, 'Come, let me show you something magical inside those leaves.'"},{"label":"problem","text":"That afternoon, Ishika noticed that the leaves in the shadiest corner of the garden looked darker and less bright green than the leaves in the sunny part. She wondered if the green colour had something to do with sunlight. 'Is the sun making the leaves green?' she asked herself."},{"label":"mathMoment","text":"Her grandmother explained: inside every leaf, there are tiny parts called chlorophyll that are green in colour. These chlorophyll parts catch sunlight and use its energy to make food for the plant. The more sunlight the leaf gets, the more bright and fresh the green colour looks, because the chlorophyll is working hard and staying healthy. The leaves are green because chlorophyll absorbs all the other colours of sunlight, like red and blue, to use for making food — and it reflects back only the green colour, which our eyes see."},{"label":"wrapUp","text":"Ishika ran to the sunny side of the garden and touched the bright green leaves, then compared them to the darker leaves in the shade. Now she understood: the leaves were green because of chlorophyll doing its job, and sunlight made them look their brightest green. She told her grandmother, 'So the green colour is like the plant's way of saying it is healthy and making its own food!' Her grandmother hugged her and said, 'Exactly right, beta.'"}],"visual":null,"equations":[],"tryTogether":{"question":"You have a plant at home with some leaves in bright sunlight and some in shadow. Which leaves do you think will look a brighter, fresher green colour, and why?","answer":"The leaves in bright sunlight will look brighter and fresher green, because the chlorophyll in them is catching more sunlight energy to make food, so it stays healthy and reflects back a stronger green colour."},"parentPrompt":"Take Ishika to a plant or tree together and compare the colour of leaves in sun versus shade. Ask her what she notices."}
